WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.710
Hey everyone this is Jared, creator

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.480
of 8-bit Philosophy and we’ve got a special
episode for you guys. Now while many games

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.840
are certainly philosophical—very few are
as effective at integrating philosophical

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.710
concepts in to the plot as Bioshock.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:26.329
Not only is the Bioshock trilogy a pioneer
of gameplay- mixing elements of survival horror,

00:00:26.329 --> 00:00:31.460
RPGs, and FPS games, but it’s also flaunts
a level of intellectual sophistication rarely

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.809
seen in games. Welcome

00:00:32.809 --> 00:00:38.420
to this special episode on The Philosophy
of Bioshock. Oh, and we’re skipping the

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.010
second one.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:43.949
“Who is John Galt?” Is the cryptic question
posed in Atlas Shrugged, the most famous book

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:49.559
by Russian born thinker Ayn Rand. Similarly,
“Who is Atlas?” posters adorn the walls

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:53.019
of Rapture—transforming the original question
of Rand’s work.

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:58.249
In the book, John Galt, engineer, genius,
and heartthrob, encourages a strike of the

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:02.319
mind, where all the world’s industrialists
are encouraged to leave their roles in an

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:06.140
ever increasingly controlling, regulated,
and rule driven world.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.500
Frustrated with the inefficiency of Socialism,
and it’s lack of care for the individual

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.030
and their hard work, Galt retreats to Galt’s
Gulch to shut down the industrial motor of

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:16.730
the world.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:22.330
The original Bioshock takes place in Rapture,
a digital homage to Galt’s Gulch, in which

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:27.250
all the world’s best minds were brought
together undersea by one man- Andrew Ryan.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:32.140
The names Andrew Ryan is effectively an anagram
for “Ayn Rand” (Well. More or less.)

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:37.120
Andrew Ryan’s political philosophy is lifted
right from Rand’s concept of “objectivism.”

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.710
Objectivism is a sort of guide to living a
worthy life—its main tenants are: reality,

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.970
reason, self-interest and capitalism.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.590
Basically, Rand believes that Reality is concrete
and objective—there’s no point in talking

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.910
about the mystical, divine, and supernatural
as guiding forces for humanity—actions should

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:58.730
be determined solely on the basis of intellect.
An easier way to say this is: Ayn Rand is

00:01:58.730 --> 00:01:59.640
an atheist.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.360
And apparently so is Andrew Ryan:

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:08.530
Ryan: I believe in no God – no invisible
man in the sky. But there is something more

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:14.530
powerful than each of us: a combination of
our efforts, a Great Chain of industry that

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:14.989
unites us.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:20.239
Andrew Ryan serves as a mouthpiece for Rand’s
condemnation of coercive governments in favor

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:21.659
of a society based in belief that coercive
government policies violate people’s autonomy.

00:02:21.659 --> 00:02:23.640
Her reaction is an ethics if egoism or radical
self interest. Objecivism. It holds that people

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.840
ought to work hard to achieve happiness in
their own lives, work hard, and not treat

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.459
others are mere means to an end.

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:27.599
Andrew Ryan’s Monologues might as well have
been lifted directly from Rand’s work:

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:29.049
Ryan: Though my physical defenses fall, you'll
not defeat me. My strength is not in steel

00:02:29.049 --> 00:02:29.120
and fire, but in my intellect and will. You
hear me, Atlas? Andrew Ryan offers you nothing

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.359
but ashes! In the end, all that matters to
me is me. And all that matters to you is you.

00:02:30.359 --> 00:02:30.819
It is the nature of things.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:34.439
Rapture was founded under the belief that
the communist parasites in the world above

00:02:34.439 --> 00:02:39.700
were freeloaders that stole, violated, and
unjustly took from the captains if industry.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.689
Ryan was tired of working and not getting
what he deserved—just like Galt.

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:50.609
Ryan: “No,” says the man in Washington,
“It belongs to the poor.” “No,” says

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:59.010
the man in the Vatican, “It belongs to God.”
“No,” says the man in Moscow, “It belongs

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.019
to everyone.” I rejected those answers.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:08.269
Also like Ryan, Rand was a proponent of Laissez-faire
capitalism or, the idea that a good government

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:12.519
exists only to protect individual rights,
and stays out of people’s personal lives

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:15.260
and the market.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:22.260
Similarly, in the world of Rapture, The plasmid
market is severely under regulated—the belief

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.689
is that the unfettered market provides the
best options for consumers.

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:30.909
Ryan (tape): There has been tremendous pressure
to regulate this plasmid business. There have

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:39.730
been side effects: blindness, insanity, death.
But what use is our ideology if it is not

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:47.230
tested? The market does not respond like an
infant, shrieking at the first sign of displeasure.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:51.029
The market is patient, and we must be too.

00:03:51.029 --> 00:03:55.540
So what went wrong? Does the ruinous state
of Rapture suggest that a society run by such

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.150
tenants is doomed to fail? Or did Ryan pervert
Rand’s beliefs?

00:03:59.809 --> 00:04:05.349
This is where ADAM comes in. ADAM is a resource
so potent that it led to a civil war between

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:10.620
Ryan and Fontaine, err… Atlas. Due to Ryan’s
ideals of a free market economy, ADAM was

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.730
completely deregulated, creating a population
of addicts riddled with side effects-— resulting

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.690
in rampant chaos and instability

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.060
Using the little sisters as mules
for magical sea slugs to create ADAM certainly

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:43.740
treats them as mere tools for your own goals.
And Ms. Rand would certainly disapprove.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.229
Lets be clear—the trendy discussion on Bioshock
Infinite has something to do with quantum

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:57.250
entanglement and multiverses— and while
it's a great discussion—it’s already been

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:02.319
covered ad nauseam. We are going to abbreviate
the discussion because… science is hard.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.449
The third installment of the Bioshock series
moves from the underwater world of Rapture

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:09.560
to the floating city: Columbia.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.530
A play on the United States Capital, Columbia
is a sort of floating Eden founded by a man

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.729
named Zachary Hale Comstock.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:21.470
Comstock is a fictional stand in for nineteenth
century New York political figure Anthony

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:26.360
Comstock—famous for his fight against obscenity
and his rather prudish values. The Comstock

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.819
character mirrors Anthony’s public feud
with noted anarchist Emma Goldman, who is

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:32.849
doubled by Daisy Fitzroy.

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:37.560
Comstock founds Columbia with the promise
of providing a place for God’s people, apart

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:49.870
from the world of sinners in the Sodom below.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:54.729
This image of Columbia as a shining beacon
in the sky has its philosophical justification

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:59.289
in the concept of American Exceptionalism.
American Exceptionalism is the belief that

00:05:59.289 --> 00:06:04.150
the New World was promised to God’s chosen
people. And through this philosophy, Bioshock

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:07.340
Infinite provides us with a tasty satire of
America’s Historical landscape.

00:06:09.099 --> 00:06:14.220
American Exceptionalism is a way of justifying
a particular lifestyle. Columbia, Like America,

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.229
is considered God’s country. Forgiveness
of sins, Salvation, and Baptism are all religious

00:06:19.229 --> 00:06:33.930
themes in Infinite—but like it’s predecessor,
this game explores the dark side of this philosophy.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.750
Similar to Comstock’s Colombia, The expansion
into the new frontier was justified as a mission

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:44.220
from God—to ensure that the new world doesn’t
backslide like the old world sinners of Europe.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:49.590
America was to be a land populated by exceptional,
chosen people, exempt, and above the rest

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:56.039
of the world. Secularized as manifest destiny—American
Excpetionalism justifies and demands the extermination

00:06:56.039 --> 00:07:00.860
of the indigenous people of the “wilderness,”
AKA Native Americans, for the sake of securing

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.259
the national identity of the Christian settlers.
Those not willing to accept Christianity were

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:07.900
forcibly moved or eliminated.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:11.979
American Exceptionalism is not just a question
of land—the ideology of this philosophy

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:17.639
is inextricably woven into the fabric of history.
Slavery, labor strikes, the abuses of Irish

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:21.979
and Chinese workers who built the American
Railroad system—are all justified by the

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:25.460
lofty goal of a promised city for God’s
people.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:30.419
Fearful of the anarchist Vox Populi, afraid
of the Irish and Chinese threat, and scared

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:40.780
of racial mixing, Columbia is a paranoid place.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:44.889
Infinite explores America’s haunted past—The
Battle of Wounded knee which is considered

00:07:44.889 --> 00:07:52.199
the end of the U.S and Indian wars, is heralded.
Laws enforcing racial segregation for relationship

00:07:52.199 --> 00:08:00.530
and marriage, and lynching are not just the
norm—they’re commonplace. Perhaps this

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:28.909
is why the enemies in the games are robotic
George Washington’s called “Patriots.”

00:08:28.909 --> 00:08:32.419
With Bioshock Infinite we get the prospect
that the Hero, Booker Dewitt is both Comstock

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:33.860
and Andrew Ryan—when it is revealed that
in another dimension the hero of the game

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:38.219
is simultaneously the villain. Bioshock exposes
the paranoia of an Us versus Tthem Dichotomy—it

00:08:38.219 --> 00:08:43.240
challenges our conception of good guys and
bad guys—of freedom fighters, revolutionaries,

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:52.899
dictators and terrorists.

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:58.260
Kill little sisters or save them, throw a
ball at an interracial couple or at the racist

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:03.160
announcer, accept or reject the baptismal
forgiveness of sins.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:09.100
One of the essential dilemmas of Bioshock
is the freedom of action, choice, and free

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:13.670
will. For those of you that don’t know me,
my name is Matt and I run a channel called

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:20.180
Game Theorists. Thanks a lot to Wisecrack
for having me. Let’s get in to it- this

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:24.079
is the importance of choice and Free Will
in Bioshock.

00:09:24.079 --> 00:09:30.100
In the first Bioshock we are told: “Andrew
Ryan reminds us – we all make choices, but,

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:37.850
in the end, our choices make us.” So…
if we make choices, we must be free. But then

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:42.950
again, if our choices make us then how can
we be free to make them? This off conundrum

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:46.519
leads us to the question: Is Free will even
possible?

00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:51.250
In Oone school of thought known as, Hard Determinists
suggests believe that there is no such thing

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:56.329
as free will. If all action can be boiled
down to cause and effect, then all of our

00:09:56.329 --> 00:09:59.560
actions are always bound by an external force.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:04.370
Even you, viewer, may think you are free to
click off this video right now. But keep in

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:09.000
mind that if you do, there is a REASON you
are doing so. So the question is:, are you

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:14.310
truly free in your actions, or are your actions
a result of a series of events that you had

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:19.440
nothing to do with? Our “choice” in our
actions is merely an illusion.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:27.260
Andrew Ryan reiterates this with the phrase
“A man chooses. A slave obeys.” In what

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:32.670
is perhaps the most brilliant part of the
original game, Andrew Ryan makes a statement

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:37.870
on the nature of gaming itself, and how you,
as a gamer, are destined to mindlessly follow

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:42.430
the fixed path of the game’s narrative.
And “interactivity,” or the idea that

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:47.620
you as the gamer have any bearing on how the
game will turn out, is merely an illusion.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:52.070
Infinite presents a possible way out of the
problem of free will—the multiverse—an

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:55.810
infinite number of worlds to choose from.
The fact that Booker is able to go back and

00:10:55.810 --> 00:11:01.000
choose to not be baptized—to smother baby
Comstock in his crib seems to be a way out

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:09.310
of a deterministic universe.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:12.060
Except that this very choice isn’t a real
possibility.

00:11:12.519 --> 00:11:16.589
Even when it seems that there is choice for
the player—the game hints at the problem

00:11:16.589 --> 00:11:17.709
of free will.

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:23.339
The first choice—to flip a coin- is quite
interesting. You are prompted with the “choice”

00:11:23.339 --> 00:11:28.589
to press X and flip the coin. Except,
in reality, you are not free to not flip—if

00:11:28.589 --> 00:11:33.880
you never ‘press X’, the game doesn’t
progress, it just stays there. But if you

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:38.510
do ‘press X’ to flip… well it always
ends up heads.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:42.209
Or Consider the scene in which Booker is
prompted with the choice to “Bring [them]

00:11:42.209 --> 00:11:46.899
the girl and wipe away the debt.” You are
free to walk around the room. The game even

00:11:46.899 --> 00:11:52.079
presents you with the OPTION of pressing X
to hand Elizabeth over. Yet, the only way

00:11:52.079 --> 00:12:00.199
to progress in the game isn to press square
and pick baby Elizabeth up.

00:12:00.199 --> 00:12:04.870
Even though “Press X” is an OPTION
that you can seemingly deny, the game proves

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:11.889
that this “choice” is merely an illusion.
You are fated to hand Elizabeth over. Whereas

00:12:11.889 --> 00:12:17.990
most games would just show this event in a
cut scene, Bioshock makes you press square.

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:23.480
It forces you to recognize that you are in
fact not free, and anything that indicates

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:25.970
otherwise, is merely an illusion.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:30.820
Perhaps the only way to truly assert your
freedom in the world of Bioshock is to simply

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.709
turn off the game.

